# ============================================================================
#  profiles/16.env — the whole platform on a 16 GiB laptop
# ============================================================================
#  Sourced by `make up-16`.
#
#  Every subsystem is present. Nothing is switched off — that is the point of
#  this profile and the reason it replaced an earlier `up-light` that simply
#  omitted ClickHouse and left six stacks' smoke tests skipping.
#
#  What changes is how much each container may take, and the warehouse
#  topology: four nodes instead of eight, as two shards of two replicas.
#  Sharding and replication both survive, which is what the lessons in steps 3
#  and 4 rest on.
#
#  Measured total: roughly 13.8 GiB in use. Set Docker to 16 GiB and expect
#  it to be comfortable rather than roomy.
# ============================================================================

# ---- the warehouse ---------------------------------------------------------
# Two shards, two replicas, one Keeper. The distributed-JOIN trap, the
# sharding key and the replica-failure drill all still apply.
CLICKHOUSE_PROFILE=clickhouse-4

# 1.25 GiB rather than 2. ClickHouse claims a fraction of its cgroup rather
# than the whole thing, so the tracked limit is what matters — see the ratio
# below.
CLICKHOUSE_MEM_LIMIT=1250m

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Why 0.9 and not the default 0.7
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ClickHouse tracks 70% of its cgroup by default, leaving headroom for the
#  allocations it makes outside the tracked pool. At a 2 GiB limit that was
#  1.16 GiB — and a FINAL over 22,000 orders exceeded it:
#
#      Code: 241. (total) memory limit exceeded:
#      current RSS: 1.16 GiB, maximum: 1.16 GiB
#
#  At 1.25 GiB the default ratio would leave 875 MiB, which is worse. Raising
#  it to 0.9 gives about 1.1 GiB tracked — close to what the 2 GiB node had,
#  with less spare for the untracked part.
#
#  That is a real trade, and the spill setting below is what makes it safe:
#  a query that would have been killed now writes to disk instead.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLICKHOUSE_MEMORY_RATIO=0.9

# A large GROUP BY spills to disk rather than failing. Slower, and slow is the
# right failure mode for a small node.
CLICKHOUSE_MAX_BYTES_BEFORE_EXTERNAL_GROUP_BY=400000000
CLICKHOUSE_MAX_MEMORY_USAGE=900000000

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Values with spaces are quoted, and must be
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  These files are SOURCED by the shell, not parsed by Compose. An unquoted
#
#      KAFKA_HEAP_OPTS=-Xms256m -Xmx512m
#
#  makes the shell read the space as a separator and try to run -Xmx512m as a
#  command. It prints "not found", carries on, and leaves the variable holding
#  only the first half — so the JVM gets a minimum heap and no maximum, and
#  nothing says so.
#
#  A .env file read by Compose would not need the quotes. These are not that.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Kafka -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Four brokers still. A JVM allocates plenty outside its heap, so the
# container limit sits well above it — a broker at 90% of its limit would trip
# ContainerNearMemoryLimit and turn a healthy run red.
KAFKA_HEAP_OPTS="-Xms256m -Xmx512m"
KAFKA_MEM_LIMIT=1g
CONNECT_HEAP_OPTS="-Xms256m -Xmx768m"
CONNECT_MEM_LIMIT=1200m

# ---- monitoring ------------------------------------------------------------
PROMETHEUS_MEM_LIMIT=768m
GRAFANA_MEM_LIMIT=384m
CADVISOR_MEM_LIMIT=256m

# ---- orchestration ---------------------------------------------------------
# Four Airflow components plus its database and exporter. Each component is
# mostly idle here: the work happens in the containers they start.
AIRFLOW_MEM_LIMIT=768m

# ---- Spark -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Four workers, not two. They reserve rather than consume — a worker handling
# this much data sits at a few hundred MiB — and four is what makes the
# partition split legible.
#
# Worker memory is what a worker OFFERS; executor memory is what one asks for,
# and it defaults to 1 GiB regardless. Lowering one without the other changes
# nothing, which is why both are here.
SPARK_WORKER_MEMORY=1g
SPARK_EXECUTOR_MEMORY=768m
SPARK_DRIVER_MEMORY=768m
SPARK_CORES_MAX=4

# ---- storage and dashboards ------------------------------------------------
MINIO_MEM_LIMIT=512m
SUPERSET_MEM_LIMIT=1g
